Three requirements, sized before anything is built.

  a support copilot over ten years of tickets
    4,000 requests/day  x  3 calls  =  36.0M in, 3.00M out
    cost      $7.20/day   $216/month   $0.0018/request
    index     2,700,000 chunks   16.6 GB of vectors   $22 to build once
    shape     5.4s per request, 0.8 concurrent at peak

  a nightly contract-risk enricher
    50,000 requests/day  x  2 calls  =  600.0M in, 40.00M out
    cost      $114.00/day   $3,420/month   $0.0023/request
    index     1,250,000 chunks   7.7 GB of vectors   $10 to build once
    shape     4.0s per request, 2.3 concurrent at peak

  a regulated advisory assistant
    300 requests/day  x  6 calls  =  7.2M in, 0.54M out
    cost      $1.40/day   $42/month   $0.0047/request
    index     160,000 chunks   1.0 GB of vectors   $1 to build once
    shape     13.2s per request, 0.1 concurrent at peak

Read what each number decides, because that is the point of doing this before
drawing anything.

Start with the shape column, because it is the one that surprises people. The
busiest of these three needs 2.3 requests in flight at peak. Not thirty. Not a
cluster. One container with a warm index would serve all three of these
systems at once and spend most of its day idle.

That is the normal case for internal software, and sizing is how you find out
before you have designed for a load you will never see. The architecture that
wins here is the boring one, and the argument for it is arithmetic.

Then read cost. The nightly enricher is $3,420 a month and the regulated
assistant is $42. Those two systems have nothing in common: one is a
throughput problem where a batch API and a queue are the whole design, and the
other is a governance problem where cost will never be the argument. Knowing
which you have, before the meeting, is most of what this exercise buys.

And read the index separately. The support copilot's 2.7M chunks are 17GB of
vectors — a fixed cost, a storage decision, and usually where the surprise is.
It is also the number that decides whether §12.2's approximate index is
necessary or premature.

Three habits that make an envelope useful rather than decorative:

  size the index separately     it is fixed cost and storage, not throughput
  use Little's law for shape    concurrency is arrival rate times duration,
                                and it selects the deployment target
  put the per-request cost      a monthly total is an argument; a per-request
  beside the monthly one        figure is a design constraint

(The rates above are placeholders. Substitute your provider's and every number
becomes yours — the arithmetic does not change.)
